  "response": "Investigators measured the robot's contact forces using an instrumented force and pressure measurement device, and those readings show that the measured contact force exceeded the ISO/TS 15066 limit for the hand region. The measured peak contact force was twice the permissible limit for the hand, and it is that ratio which establishes the overshoot of the hand-region threshold. In other words, the contact force recorded during the incident was 200 percent of the allowable hand force value. Because the hand-region threshold had been exceeded, investigators then compared the torque sensors' calibration records against the manufacturer's specifications. That comparison confirms that all of the robot's joint torque sensors were within calibration tolerance at the time of the incident. On the hardware question the record admits only two mutually exclusive readings: either every joint torque sensor stood within calibration tolerance when the worker was injured, or one of the robot's joint torque sensors was reading outside calibration tolerance at that moment; one of the two must hold, and they cannot hold together. Resolving that fork determines whether the excessive force originated in the sensing chain itself or in the parameters imposed upon it.\n\nThe content of the applicable technical specification must also be settled. ISO/TS 15066 specifies biomechanical force and pressure limits for collaborative robot applications. ISO/TS 15066 prohibits any physical contact between a collaborative robot and a human worker. A specification that fixes numerical ceilings for contact loading and one that bars contact outright cannot both govern the same operation, and the investigator must determine which reading controls the threshold applied to the hand before any measured value can be characterised as compliant or excessive.\n\nThe normative allocation of duties forms a second pillar of the analysis. ISO 10218-2 assigns sole responsibility for safety parameter configuration to the robot manufacturer. Investigators accordingly obtain the full integration file: the application risk assessment, the commissioning protocol, the validation report for the collaborative workspace, and the change history of the safety controller, with the identity and privilege level of every account that altered a protective parameter.\n\nThe manufacturer attributed the injury to the integrator's configuration of the safety parameters. The plant operator, by contrast, attributed the injury to a defect in the robot's force-limiting sensors. Because each account locates the fault at the opposite end of the supply chain, the investigator must secure physical and documentary evidence that does not depend on the good faith of either party, sealing the controller, its parameter set and its event log before any party is permitted to service the cell.\n\nA worker sustained a crush injury to the hand during a collaborative operation with the robot. The injury occurred after the collaborative robot had already been installed on the plant's assembly line by a third-party system integrator. An inquiry of this kind therefore proceeds on two tracks at once: the reconstruction of the physical event, and the reconstruction of the engineering decisions that preceded it, each documented to a standard that will survive adversarial scrutiny.",
